cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or benchmark, please cite the paper below."
type: software
title: "Lost in Localization: Benchmarking Intralingual Cultural Adaptation in Large Language Models"
abstract: >
  Cultural adaptation, rewriting text for a different cultural audience,
  requires more than lexical substitution. A model must decide what to
  change, whether each replacement is locally valid, and whether it
  preserves the original item's functional role. We formalize this as
  function-preserving cultural transfer and introduce a dataset, a
  taxonomy, and an evaluation framework constructed around that principle.
  CulturalAdapt-6K identifies 6,000 culturally rich texts in four discourse
  domains, while the Cultural Rewriting and Adaptation Failure Taxonomy
  (CRAFT) classifies errors into five categories. To mitigate the
  Anglocentric priors of current models, our proposed framework anchors
  each culturally significant item in a Wikidata-linked function frame,
  capturing its contextual role rather than surface denotation. Across
  191,968 adaptations by eight instruction-tuned LLMs, we find that the
  models that substitute the most are not the ones that substitute best.
  High localization increases failure exposure, while conservative
  retention increases validity scores, yet both converge to the same
  overall functional quality. Current generative models can certainly
  dress text in a target culture's vocabulary but ultimately cannot
  reliably preserve the contextual roles the vocabulary is meant to serve.
